/*  Settling a circuit — and the reason a circuit is a VALUE rather than a PIPELINE.

    ★★★ THIS FILE IS THE ARGUMENT. A circuit looks like a pipeline: signals go in one end and
    come out the other, and every gate is a little function you could imagine running as its
    own task with its inputs and outputs as channels. That picture is wrong, and the reason
    is FEEDBACK.

    A latch is two gates wired into each other's inputs. There is no "one end" — the output
    is an input. A pipeline cannot express that: pushing a value forward assumes forward
    exists, and in a circuit with a cycle it does not. Worse, a gate COMBINES all of its
    inputs, while anything that waits on several channels takes whichever ARRIVES and
    ignores the rest. Those are opposite operations, so the machinery that looks like it
    would help here is machinery that cannot.

    What works instead is the oldest idea in simulation: hold the whole state, recompute
    every gate from it AT ONCE, write the results back, and repeat until nothing changes.
    A circuit is a value you evaluate to a fixed point. Cycles are then not a special case —
    they are just a circuit that takes more rounds to settle, or never settles at all, and
    the second one is a real hardware behaviour this simulator has to be able to report.

    ⚠ Every gate is evaluated from the SAME snapshot before any result is written back. Doing
    it gate-by-gate would make the answer depend on the order the parts happen to sit in the
    catalogue, which is a bug that hides for a long time: most circuits give the same answer
    either way, and the ones that do not are exactly the latches.
*/

// How many rounds before a circuit is declared to be oscillating. A settling network
// converges in about as many rounds as its longest chain of gates is deep; a 4-bit ripple
// adder is around a dozen, so this leaves a wide margin before crying wolf.
Define settle-limit as 100 permanently.

// ── One round: what every gate is driving, from one snapshot ──────────────
//
// ★ Several gates may drive the SAME wire, which is not an error — it is a bus. They are
// combined with the wire-resolution table, so parked drivers (z) yield and disagreeing
// drivers (0 against 1) produce contention (x). This is the one place `resolved` is used,
// and it is why `z` exists at all.

Bind map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) to driving, given (
        the catalogue of (and-gate or or-gate or xor-gate or nand-gate or not-gate or tri-gate) parts,
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) wires):
    Define driven as a map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating).
    For each part in parts, repeat:
        Define name as cast driven-wire on (part).
        Define value as cast driven-value on (part, wires).
        Define already as the entry for name in driven.
        If already is not void,
            In driven, the entry for name becomes cast resolved on (already, value).
        If already is void,
            In driven, the entry for name becomes value.
    Done.
    Return driven.
Done.

// ── A private copy of the caller's inputs ─────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ MEASURED, and not what a reader coming from the rest of Cufet will expect: A MAP IS
// SHARED, NOT COPIED. Handing one to a function lets that function change the caller's, and
// `Define copy as original.` makes a second name for the same map rather than a second map.
// Objects and records copy; a map does not.
//
// So the settling loop cannot simply write into the map it was given — it would rewrite the
// caller's inputs as a side effect, and the second call with "the same" inputs would be
// driving a circuit that had already run. Building a fresh map entry by entry is the fix,
// and it has to be written out, because there is no copying form that would do it.

Bind map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) to taken-apart, given (
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) wires):
    Define fresh as a map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating).
    For each pair in wires, repeat:
        In fresh, the entry for (the key of pair) becomes the value of pair.
    Done.
    Return fresh.
Done.

// ── The fixed point ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Bind map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) to settled, given (
        the catalogue of (and-gate or or-gate or xor-gate or nand-gate or not-gate or tri-gate) parts,
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) inputs):
    Define current as cast taken-apart on (inputs).
    Define rounds as 0.
    Define moving as true.

    While moving and rounds is less than settle-limit, repeat:
        Define driven as cast driving on (parts, current).
        The moving becomes false.
        For each pair in driven, repeat:
            Define before as cast on-wire on (current, the key of pair).
            If not cast same-signal on (before, the value of pair),
                The moving becomes true.
            In current, the entry for (the key of pair) becomes the value of pair.
        Done.
        The rounds becomes rounds + 1.
    Done.

    /*  Still moving at the limit means the circuit oscillates. A ring of an odd number of
        inverters is the honest example: it has no stable state to reach, and real hardware
        does the same thing — it rings, at a frequency set by how fast the gates are.

        ⚠ NOT the latch with both inputs asserted, which is what this comment said first.
        That state SETTLES here, with both outputs high, which is also what the hardware
        does; the famous oscillation happens when both inputs are RELEASED AT ONCE, and a
        fixed-point model has no transitions, so it cannot show it. Worth stating plainly
        rather than quietly demonstrating something easier.

        ★ Only the wires STILL CHANGING become x. The rest of the circuit may well have
        settled perfectly, and turning all of it unknown would throw away the true part of
        the answer to report the false part. That is the same instinct the truth tables
        encode — `0 and x` is 0, because a known input is still knowledge. */
    If moving:
        Define ringing as cast driving on (parts, current).
        For each pair in ringing, repeat:
            Define before as cast on-wire on (current, the key of pair).
            If not cast same-signal on (before, the value of pair),
                In current, the entry for (the key of pair) becomes a new unknown { }.
        Done.
    Done.

    Return current.
Done.

// ── Building the inputs, and reading the answer ───────────────────────────

Bind map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) to wired, given (
        the series of text names,
        the series of number digits):
    Define wires as a map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating).
    // ⚠ This counter is called `slot` and not `at`, which is the name it wants. `at` is NOT a
    // reserved word — `Define at as 1.` is perfectly legal — but `item at of digits` is
    // refused, because `item at (row, column) of m` is how a matrix is indexed and the parser
    // commits to that reading on the word alone. A contextual word is given back everywhere
    // except the one position it is contextual in, which is the position you wanted it.
    Define slot as 1.
    For each name in names, repeat:
        In wires, the entry for name becomes cast from-digit on (item slot of digits).
        The slot becomes slot + 1.
    Done.
    Return wires.
Done.

Bind text to reading, given (
        the map from text to (low or high or unknown or floating) wires,
        the series of text names):
    Define shape as "".
    For each name in names, repeat:
        The shape becomes shape joined to cast shown on (cast on-wire on (wires, name)).
    Done.
    Return shape.
Done.
